nodecode-town is a rewrite of sxuff's sxuff/hermes-town:

  https://github.com/sxuff/hermes-town
  commit f3b89401fafde0cd3ebaaccf01ea9e658bc66ff9
  MIT License, Copyright (c) 2026 Hermes Town contributors (LICENSE)

The original is a passive bridge: ten lifecycle hooks publish a bounded,
pseudonymous event stream to a small Node server bundled in the plugin folder,
and a prebuilt Phaser front end draws one pixel-art resident per execution
context and walks it to a building per tool call. This rewrite keeps the idea
and the privacy rule on Nodecode's own seams: the :frame and :tool hook
points read turn facts and tool names from the running image, a bounded town
in memory holds the residents, /town answers it as text, a route serves the
page from the gateway's own port, and a snapshot route publishes the same
JSON the page polls. A resident key is still an HMAC-shaped pseudonym of the
session id under a per-home salt, and still the only identifier downstream.

Left out, and why: the bundled Node server, its bridge token, its HTTP
ingest, the SSE stream and the Phaser bundle (Nodecode has a gateway, an
in-process hook seam and a route table, so no second server or build step is
needed, and nothing is bundled); the ten Hermes hook names, collapsed to the
two points Nodecode publishes; the cron-jobs seeding, which read the
scheduler's own jobs file (Nodecode's sessions and turns are in the image
already); roles read from a child agent's role tag, which Nodecode does not
carry, replaced by the one distinction its turns have, top-level or
delegated; and the pixel-art asset set, redrawn as a canvas of rectangles and
figures in the served page.
